ng his true  identity  rather the riddler is lamenting the fact  that together the two of them took down  every one of his targets except wayne  nashton isn't talking to batman as an  adversary but as a partner after all  they're both vengeful angry men who  suffered in their youth and want to root  out corruption and see justice served  this moment and its implications are the  beginning of the end for bruce relying  entirely on fear and violence he sees  the limits of what he has done and worse  sees the evil his war on crime has  inadvertently inspired  another revelation takes place within  arkham's walls before the final credits  begin to roll as nashton has a meltdown  in his cell knowing that batman has  thwarted his plans a friendly cell  neighbor comforts him through their  interaction it becomes pretty clear that  the individual nashton is befriending is  supposed to be the joker however what's  confusing is barry kyogen's casting fans  assume that he'll be playing the joker  in some kind of post-credits scene this  scene taking place before the film's  actual closing moments is a bit of a  shock but largely because it begs the  question of how the joker ended up in  arkham is he already the joker did  batman catch him in a previous adventure  do we really need another live-action  joker so soon after joaquin phoenix's  oscar-winning turn with the character  only time will tell if he shows up in a  potential sequel  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  the batman are coming soon subscribe to  our youtube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one

